---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const steps = [
  { n: "01", t: "Shade",    s: ["Covered for the last", "20 to 40 days"] },
  { n: "02", t: "Pick",     s: ["Youngest leaves,", "first harvest only"] },
  { n: "03", t: "Steam",    s: ["Within hours,", "to stop oxidation"] },
  { n: "04", t: "Dry",      s: ["Cooled, then", "dried flat"] },
  { n: "05", t: "Destem",   s: ["Stem and vein out.", "This is tencha"] },
  { n: "06", t: "Grind",    s: ["Granite, slowly.", "30 to 40g an hour"] },
];

const essays = [
  {
    title: "My Lifelong Career as a Reader and Writer",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 6, page 26",
    href: "/writing/reader-and-writer/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2026/03/Translingual-Issue-6.pdf",
    note: "How reading arrived secondhand, from a brother whose books I was given after him, and what it took to make it mine.",
  },
  {
    title: "A Film Analysis: Grave of the Fireflies",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 16",
    href: "/writing/grave-of-the-fireflies/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "An antiwar film that haunts without a single scare, and why that stays with a person far longer than fright does.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shades of Black",
    where: "Translingual, Issue 2, page 42",
    href: "/writing/shades-of-black/",
    published: "https://ielp.ehe.osu.edu/files/2023/04/Translingual-V1.-I2.-Final.pdf",
    note: "Someone said I dressed like Dracula. The colour turned out to be doing more work than either of us assumed.",
  },
  {
    title: "Imprisonment in The Allegory of the Cave and The House of Asterion",
    where: "Unpublished",
    href: "/writing/imprisonment/",
    note: "Plato and Borges arriving at the same question from opposite ends: whether someone who cannot see the walls is still imprisoned by them.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Rough to Cut"
  description="The inspirations behind this site. Matcha as a morning ritual, semi-precious stones, and four essays. By Linh (Min) Le."
>
  <article class="rough">

    <header class="band g-cream rough-head">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beyond work</p>
        <h1>Rough to Cut</h1>
        <p class="rough-lede">
          Curious about inspirations behind this site? Green and soft cream palettes running through
          every page. Fragments that form into a polished stone at first landing. And
          longer-than-necessary narratives under each work. They are intentional, yes, guided by
          instinct and shaped by fragments of what interests me outside of work.
        </p>
        <p class="rough-sub">
          Nothing professional below the line. Each piece, still, is a piece of my identity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell rough-split">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Matcha</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>It took me a while to work out that the calm is the precision</h2>

        <p>
          I make it every morning, and for a long time I assumed the point was the drink. It is
          mostly the procedure. Most people unwind by doing something loose, and what settles me is
          a sequence with a right answer, where the order matters and my attention has exactly one
          place to be. Twenty minutes of that before the day has made any claim on me, and I start
          out steadier than I otherwise would. There is a full set at home, the bowl and the whisk,
          and a corner of the kitchen that does nothing else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some of the care I am borrowing was spent long before the tin ever reached me, which is
          the part that keeps me reading about it. The plants are covered for the last twenty to
          forty days before picking, and being starved of light they overcompensate, flooding
          themselves with chlorophyll and turning their catechins into L-theanine, which is where
          the colour and the sweetness both come from. Only the youngest leaves of the first harvest
          are taken. They are steamed within hours to halt oxidation, cooled, dried, then stripped
          of stem and vein until only leaf flesh remains, and graded before a single gram is ground.
          Then it goes between two granite stones turning slowly enough to yield thirty or forty
          grams in an hour, because speed makes heat and heat takes the aroma with it.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">Nothing in that survives being rushed, which is a strange thing to find comforting in a powder.</p>
        <p>
          The ceremony asks the same of me at a far smaller scale, and that part is mine. Water off
          the boil rather than at it. The ratio measured rather than guessed. A whisk moved fast
          enough to raise a foam and gently enough to spare the bowl. It is exacting work with a low
          ceiling on how badly it can go, which is a rare combination and a restful one, and it is
          the closest thing I have to a reset button.
        </p>
        <p>
          What arrives afterwards is the other half of why I keep at it. Matcha carries L-theanine
          alongside its caffeine, so what I get is clarity rather than a jolt: alert, level, and
          still level three hours later. Coffee lends me an hour and then collects on the debt,
          usually in the middle of the afternoon when I can least afford it.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/matcha-whisk.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="A bamboo chasen lifted out of a white ceramic bowl, matcha whisked to a flat green surface." />
          <figcaption>The whisk is bamboo, cut into a hundred tines, and it is the only tool in the process I own.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-leaf" style="--plate: var(--g-ivory);">
      <div class="shell rough-split mirror">
        <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Stones</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>I collect the ones nobody has agreed on</h2>

        <p>
          One of the first places I went looking after moving to Cincinnati was a rock shop.
          GeoRarities is a suite inside an art studio building and announces itself to nobody, which
          is most of why I liked it, and I still go. Almost everything I own came out of small
          places like it.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I collect is semi-precious, a phrase worth pausing on, because it describes a stone
          that has been ranked and found to be worth less. That ranking is most of the appeal. When
          something is expensive the price has already done the admiring and left you nothing to
          decide. When it is cheap you are alone with the thing, and whatever it is worth has to
          come from somewhere harder to see: where it was made, how long the ground took over it,
          what people have believed about it for four thousand years. I would rather be handed that
          argument than the answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          My element in Eastern philosophy is Water, so almost everything I carry is blue or white,
          and one stone has never left the rotation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lapis lazuli is a rock rather than a single mineral, which I like about it too: lazurite
          for the blue, calcite threaded so finely that you go looking for the white, and pyrite for
          the gold. The Egyptians set it into the burial masks of their royals and ground it into
          the most expensive blue a painter could buy. People take it for sodalite, which is a fair
          mistake in a dim room and an impossible one outdoors, because sodalite carries almost no
          pyrite, and in sunlight lapis throws small gold sparks that nothing else does.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">The pyrite is technically an impurity. It is also the entire reason it looks like a night sky.</p>
        <p>
          The meaning is the part I keep it on me for. Lapis is called the stone of wisdom, and most
          tellings stop at clear thinking, which is the half I can already manage on my own. The
          half I want is what comes after it: knowing what you think, and then carrying enough
          weight that a room believes you and moves. I have spent most of my life better at the
          first than the second, and something that has been trusted for four thousand years makes
          a decent daily argument against staying that way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mark on this site is rough gathering into a cut stone, then breaking apart and
          gathering again. That was not a metaphor I went looking for.
        </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="shot">
          <img src="/media/rough/lapis-lazuli.jpg" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Tumbled lapis lazuli stones, deep blue with white calcite veining and small flecks of gold pyrite." />
          <figcaption>Blue lazurite, white calcite, and the gold pyrite that tells it apart from sodalite.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-beige" style="--plate: var(--g-leaf);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Writing</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>Before any of the analysis, there was this</h2>
        <p>
          Most of these appeared in Translingual, the journal of Ohio State&rsquo;s International
          English Language Programs. The last one I never submitted anywhere, and it is here because
          I still like it too much to leave out. All of them are readable in full, set from the
          manuscripts.
        </p>

        <ol class="essays">
          {essays.map((e) => (
            <li class="essay">
              <h3 class="essay-title"><a href={e.href}>{e.title}</a></h3>
              <p class="essay-where figure">
                {e.where}
                {e.published && (
                  <> &middot; <a href={e.published} target="_blank" rel="noopener">as published</a></>
                )}
              </p>
              <p class="essay-note">{e.note}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band plated g-leaf" style="--plate: var(--g-ivory);">
      <div class="shell prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The throughline</p>
        <h2 class="words" data-words>Fluency in one language was never the point</h2>
        <p>
          Words came easily enough that I wrote a book at fifteen. They could do nothing about
          getting anyone at school to talk to me. Being natural in written language built me a
          comfort zone, and the comfort zone quietly turned into a hiding place, because as long as
          I could put a thing well on a page I never had to carry it across to another person.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of what I have chosen since has been an argument with that. Matcha, where the whole
          quality of the thing was decided upstream by people I will never meet and my part is small
          and late and honest about being small. Lapis, kept for the moment after the thinking rather
          than the thinking itself. The essays, which taught me that a well-made sentence with
          nothing behind it is worth less than a plain one that means something. All three are about
          what happens at a boundary.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the work as well. My degree is in Information Systems and my foundation is
          accounting, and what I keep choosing is the position between a technical system and the
          people who have to decide something with it. Being proficient in Python or in finance
          guarantees nothing on its own. A recommendation that no one outside the room can follow
          has not been made yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the Spotify case I built the financial model, and the part I would defend is what
          happened around it. Behavioural science, product design and finance arrived speaking three
          languages and disagreeing about what even counted as evidence, and somebody had to carry
          one argument across all three until a stakeholder could follow it from the root cause to
          the number without being handed a translation.
        </p>
        <p class="beat">
          Which means the thing I actually do is none of those languages. It is the distance between
          them. I started out fluent in one and hid there for years, and every choice since has been
          learning to cross.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-out">
          <a class="cta ghost" href="/work/compass-ai/">The case that argument was built for</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rough-head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .rough-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .rough-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin-top: .9rem;
    padding-top: .9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  .rough-lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .rough-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 23rem);
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 3.25rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 23rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .rough-split.mirror .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; }
  .rough-split .shot { position: sticky; top: 5.5rem; margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .rough-split, .rough-split.mirror { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .rough-split.mirror .shot, .rough-split .shot { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; position: static; }
    .rough-split.mirror .prose { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
    .rough-split .shot { max-width: 34rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
  }

  .prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -.015em;
    margin: .3rem 0 1.3rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.05em; line-height: 1.62; }
  .prose .beat {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 60, "WONK" 1;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .shot { margin: 1.8rem 0 2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .7rem; }
  .shot img {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    padding-left: .75rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-line);
  }

  .fig { margin: 1.6rem 0 1.8rem; }
  .fig-frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: .8rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .fig-frame svg { display: block; width: 100%; min-width: 34rem; height: auto; }
  .st rect { fill: none; stroke: var(--rule-strong); stroke-width: 1; }
  .st.on rect { fill: var(--accent-wash); stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .sn  { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .1em; }
  .stt { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; fill: var(--ink); }
  .st.on .stt { fill: var(--accent-deep); }
  .sss { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 7.5px; fill: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cap { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 8px; fill: var(--ink-faint); letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .arw { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.2; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }

  .essays { list-style: none; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .essay { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .essay:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  .essay-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 40;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .essay-title a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-line); }
  .essay-title a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .essay-where {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .essay-note { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-top: .45rem; line-height: 1.55; }

  .closing-out { margin-top: .8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; text-decoration: none;
    background: var(--accent-deep); color: var(--g-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-deep); border-radius: 7px;
    padding: .6rem .95rem; display: inline-block;
    transition: background .25s ease, border-color .25s ease, color .25s ease;
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--ink); border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 34%, transparent); }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--accent-wash); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .cta:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
</style>
